Andrew Fabbro <andrew.fab...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> I know that a pet will not pick up a cursed item, so if the pet picks
> an item up and drops it, you know it's not cursed.

Yes.

> However, it seems that there are some items a pet won't pick up (or
> will take a really long time to pick up) that are still not cursed.

It an item is too heavy the pet will not pick it up.  Having mostly
played classes that get dogs or cats I was amazed the first time
I saw a pony pick up a plate armor in a shop.  Playing a knight
if you want to make sure your horse does not pick up a chest
you have to put a ton in it, or cover it with a cursed item.

> If a pet walks over a square with an item "only reluctantly" then it's
> cursed for sure, but what are the rules regarding pet movement and
> cursed items?

Pets avoid cursed items but it isn't complete avoidance.  It seems
to be similar to avoiding known traps - I've lost pets who stepped
under a falling rock trap twice even though they usually don't step
on a trap a second time.

> I guess I'm asking what exactly is the procedure for "pet-testing".
> I'd previously thought pet-testing was whether or not a pet picked up
> an item, but it seems that movement is the defining test...

I drop items around me and watch my pet's movements.  If the
pet moves reluctantly then I name it "cur".  If the pet steps on it
or picks it up I name it "uncur" - I do this a lot for daggers - or I
try it on and take it off - I do this a lot for armor - in the early
game.

So what if the pet dances around me without ever stepping on
it?  Judgement time, welcome to the ambiguous nature of
NetHack.  At some point I pick the item back up, name it "cur?"
as an initial guess.  I can always change the name later without
the question mark if I become certain.

Most of the time that initial guess is correct when I find out by
dragiing it to an altar.  The longer I'm willing to watch the pet
dance, the higher the percentage for the guess.


